's high of 64\u00b0F was typical for November 13.","t_yest":"November 13 was a typical day","p_yest":"The high of <strong>64\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 70\u00b0F, and the record is 87\u00b0F (1938, 1955). The low of <strong>38\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Hattiesburg has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Hattiesburg's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Hattiesburg has set <strong class=\"sig\">29 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 76 that chance alone would give, and <strong>83 record highs</strong>, close to the 77 that chance alone would give. The 1920s hold the most record highs of any decade (60). Hattiesburg's all-time high is 107\u00b0F, on August 26, 2023.","t_chg":"Hattiesburg's nights have warmed. Its days have warmed since 1986.","p_chg":"Across 111 complete years: Hattiesburg's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.14\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.07 to +0.21), about +1.8\u00b0F since 1894. Hattiesburg's average high shows no clear change: \u22120.08\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.16 to +0.00) that includes zero. It's close, but the range still includes zero. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.70\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.41 to +0.99) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.87\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.55 to +1.19). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 7 of 12 months. Highs rose in none; June and July fell.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Hattiesburg's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.07 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 59.0 inches a year. The wettest year was 1961 (90.71 in); the driest, 1963 (37.28 in). Hattiesburg almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.5 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 8 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}